sections.body:
try/catch catches a **synchronous** throw that happens somewhere within the dynamic extent of the try block -- meaning: the call stack that includes the try block has to still be executing when the throw happens, so that unwinding it can reach the matching catch. That single requirement is exactly what breaks the moment an operation becomes asynchronous:

```javascript
const fs = require('fs');

try {
  fs.readFile('./does-not-exist.txt', (err, data) => {
    if (err) throw err;   // throwing here does NOT get caught by the try/catch below
    console.log(data);
  });
} catch (err) {
  console.error('Caught it:', err.message);   // never runs
}
```

By the time fs.readFile's callback actually executes -- after the file system operation has completed, dispatched back through the event loop -- the original try block has already finished running (fs.readFile itself returned immediately, since it's non-blocking) and the stack frame containing that try/catch is long gone. Throwing inside the callback throws into whatever is calling the callback at that later point (deep inside Node's internals), which has no matching catch anywhere nearby, and the result is an uncaught exception -- covered in depth in the next concept -- not a caught one. This is precisely why the async patterns concept emphasized checking the err argument explicitly (if (err) { ... }) instead of throwing: it's not a style preference, it's the only mechanism available, because try/catch fundamentally cannot bridge this particular gap in time.

Given that a throw inside an async callback has nowhere useful to land, Node's core APIs are deliberately designed to never do that for expected, anticipated failure conditions -- they follow the error-first convention instead, passing the error object as the callback's first argument, which is the one piece of code guaranteed to run in response to the operation completing (successfully or not). This turns 'catching an error' into 'reading a value,' which works across any gap in time, since it's just an ordinary function argument rather than something depending on a live call stack:

```javascript
fs.readFile('./does-not-exist.txt', (err, data) => {
  if (err) {
    console.error('Failed to read file:', err.message);   // this DOES run
    return;
  }
  console.log(data);
});
```

The practical consequence is that error-first callback code needs its error handling written explicitly, by hand, at every callback boundary -- there is no ambient try/catch that will do it for you, and skipping the if (err) check is not a syntax error, just a silent path straight into using data that might be undefined.

Promises solve the same underlying problem -- how do you deliver a failure that happens later, across an asynchronous gap -- with a different mechanism than error-first callbacks: instead of an argument you have to remember to check, a rejected Promise automatically skips every subsequent .then() handler in its chain until it reaches a .catch() (or a second argument to .then), the same way a synchronous throw skips subsequent code until it reaches a matching catch block:

```javascript
fetchUser(id)
  .then((user) => fetchOrders(user.id))
  .then((orders) => {
    if (orders.length === 0) {
      throw new Error('No orders found');   // this throw becomes a REJECTION of the chain
    }
    return summarize(orders);
  })
  .then((summary) => console.log(summary))
  .catch((err) => console.error('Something failed:', err.message));
```

Notice the throw new Error(...) inside the second .then() handler: throwing synchronously inside a .then() callback is automatically converted into a rejection of the Promise that .then() call itself returns, which then propagates onward exactly like a rejection from fetchUser or fetchOrders would -- there's no special syntax needed to 'convert' a throw into a rejection here, .then() does it for you. A single .catch() at the end of the chain is reached regardless of *which* step actually failed, which is the specific advantage over callback style: you don't need a repeated if (err) check at every single link.

await unwraps a Promise's eventual outcome back into ordinary synchronous-looking control flow: a resolved value becomes the expression's result, and a rejection is re-thrown at that exact line, which is precisely what makes try/catch usable again inside an async function:

```javascript
async function getOrderSummary(id) {
  try {
    const user = await fetchUser(id);
    const orders = await fetchOrders(user.id);
    if (orders.length === 0) throw new Error('No orders found');
    return summarize(orders);
  } catch (err) {
    console.error('Something failed:', err.message);
    return null;
  }
}
```

The critical word there is *await*: try/catch only catches a rejection from a Promise you actually awaited (or otherwise attached a handler to) within that try block. A very common, specific mistake is forgetting the await on a call that returns a Promise, inside a try block that otherwise looks correct:

```javascript
async function getOrderSummaryBuggy(id) {
  try {
    const user = await fetchUser(id);
    const orders = fetchOrders(user.id);   // BUG: missing await
    return summarize(orders);              // summarize gets a Promise, not the real orders
  } catch (err) {
    console.error('Something failed:', err.message);   // never runs if fetchOrders rejects
    return null;
  }
}
```

Because fetchOrders(user.id) is never awaited, its Promise is handed directly to summarize -- which almost certainly breaks, but more importantly, if fetchOrders itself goes on to *reject* later, that rejection happens completely outside the try block's awareness: the try/catch has already moved past that line by the time the rejection occurs, since nothing paused for it. The rejection becomes unhandled, exactly the scenario the next section, and the next concept, deal with directly.

Every mechanism above assumes something downstream is actually paying attention -- awaiting the Promise, or attaching a .catch() to it. That assumption breaks down specifically when an async function is handed to code that has no concept of Promises at all, which describes most event-emitter-style APIs:

```javascript
const server = http.createServer(async (req, res) => {
  const data = await fetchDataThatMightReject();   // if this rejects...
  res.end(JSON.stringify(data));
});
// http.createServer's internals call this handler and do NOT await its returned
// Promise or attach a .catch() to it -- a rejection here becomes unhandled
```

http.createServer (and EventEmitter.on more generally) invokes the handler you give it and moves on -- it has no idea the function is async, doesn't know it returns a Promise, and never awaits or catches anything from it. If fetchDataThatMightReject() rejects, that rejection has nowhere to go: no try/catch inside the handler catches it (there isn't one here), and no caller awaits or catches the outer async function's own returned Promise either, since http.createServer never looks at it. The result is an unhandled promise rejection -- for that specific request, the client's connection simply hangs (res.end() never gets called), and depending on Node's version and configuration, the process itself may crash. The fix is always the same shape: wrap the body of any async function handed to a non-Promise-aware API in its own try/catch, so failures are caught locally rather than relying on a caller that was never designed to notice them.

examples.code:
const fs = require('fs');

function loadConfigBroken() {
  try {
    fs.readFile('./config.json', 'utf8', (err, data) => {
      if (err) throw err;   // throws into fs.readFile's internal completion machinery, not here
      return JSON.parse(data);
    });
  } catch (err) {
    console.log('This never runs for a missing file, even though config.json is missing');
  }
  console.log('loadConfigBroken() returned, before the file read has even finished');
}

loadConfigBroken();

function fetchUser(id) {
  return id > 0
    ? Promise.resolve({ id, name: 'Amy' })
    : Promise.reject(new Error('Invalid user id'));
}

function fetchOrders(userId) {
  return Promise.resolve([]);   // simulate: this user has no orders
}

fetchUser(-1)
  .then((user) => fetchOrders(user.id))
  .then((orders) => {
    if (orders.length === 0) throw new Error('No orders found');
    return orders.length;
  })
  .then((count) => console.log(`Order count: ${count}`))
  .catch((err) => console.error('Chain failed:', err.message));
// logs: 'Chain failed: Invalid user id' -- note fetchOrders/the length check never even run

async function processOrder(orderId) {
  try {
    console.log('Starting order processing...');
    chargeCard(orderId);   // BUG: missing await -- returns a Promise, not a result
    console.log('Charge succeeded, shipping order.');   // runs regardless of the real outcome
  } catch (err) {
    console.error('Order failed:', err.message);   // never runs, even if chargeCard rejects
  }
}

function chargeCard(orderId) {
  return Promise.reject(new Error('Card declined'));
}

processOrder(101);
// Logs 'Starting order processing...' then 'Charge succeeded, shipping order.'
// -- even though the card was actually declined -- and later, an unhandled rejection warning/crash

