sections.body:
process.on('uncaughtException', handler) fires when a synchronous throw (or a thrown error inside a callback, exactly as covered in the previous concept) is never caught by any try/catch anywhere in the call stack it propagates through. process.on('unhandledRejection', handler) fires when a Promise rejects and nothing -- no .catch(), no awaiting try/catch -- ever handles that rejection; since Node 15, an unhandled rejection crashes the process by default, the same as an uncaught exception always has.

It's tempting to read the existence of these events as an invitation to write a handler that logs the error and lets the process keep running, effectively neutralizing every crash. Node's own documentation is explicit and deliberate on this point: **'the correct use of uncaughtException is to perform synchronous cleanup of allocated resources ... it is not safe to resume normal operation after uncaughtException.'** The reasoning connects directly to the previous section: an uncaught exception, by definition, escaped every try/catch that existed in the code -- which almost always means it's a programmer error the code never anticipated, precisely the category where continuing to run risks operating on corrupted state. The legitimate use of these handlers is narrow and specific: log the error with enough detail to diagnose it later, flush any pending writes or close open connections cleanly if you can do so quickly and synchronously, and then explicitly call process.exit(1) -- not to swallow the error and pretend nothing happened.

```javascript
process.on('uncaughtException', (err) => {
  console.error('Fatal error, shutting down:', err);
  // synchronous cleanup only -- do not attempt further async work here
  process.exit(1);
});
```

Since the *meaning* of an error (operational vs programmer) isn't something JavaScript's Error type tracks for you automatically, a common, practical pattern is a custom error class that carries an explicit flag:

```javascript
class AppError extends Error {
  constructor(message, statusCode = 500) {
    super(message);
    this.name = 'AppError';
    this.statusCode = statusCode;
    this.isOperational = true;   // explicitly marks this as an anticipated failure
  }
}

// Usage in a route:
app.get('/users/:id', async (req, res, next) => {
  const user = await db.findUser(req.params.id);
  if (!user) {
    return next(new AppError('User not found', 404));   // operational -- expected, handled
  }
  res.json(user);
});

// Error-handling middleware (recognized by its 4-parameter signature, from the building_with_node module)
app.use((err, req, res, next) => {
  if (err.isOperational) {
    res.status(err.statusCode).json({ error: err.message });
  } else {
    console.error('Unexpected error, crashing for a clean restart:', err);
    res.status(500).json({ error: 'Internal server error' });
    process.exit(1);   // let a process manager restart with a known-good state
  }
});
```

The .isOperational flag turns an implicit, easy-to-get-wrong judgment call ('is this the kind of error I should recover from, or the kind that means I don't trust my own state anymore?') into an explicit, deliberate decision made at the point where the error was created -- a missing user is created as an AppError with isOperational: true because the code that created it knew, at that moment, this was an anticipated case; any other, unmarked error reaching that same middleware is treated as a signal that something genuinely unexpected happened.

examples.code:
class AppError extends Error {
  constructor(message, statusCode) {
    super(message);
    this.isOperational = true;
    this.statusCode = statusCode;
  }
}

async function getDiscountForUser(userId) {
  const user = await db.findUser(userId);
  if (!user) {
    // Anticipated: a caller might pass an id that doesn't exist -- not a bug
    throw new AppError('User not found', 404);
  }
  // If db.findUser's return shape ever silently changes and `user.tier` doesn't
  // exist, THIS throws a plain, unmarked TypeError -- a programmer error, not
  // something this function anticipated or is trying to represent on purpose
  return discountRatesByTier[user.tier.toUpperCase()];
}

const server = http.createServer(app);
server.listen(3000);

process.on('uncaughtException', (err) => {
  console.error('Uncaught exception -- shutting down:', err);
  // Stop accepting NEW connections, but let in-flight ones finish briefly
  server.close(() => {
    process.exit(1);
  });
  // Safety net: force-exit if close() takes too long (e.g. a stuck connection)
  setTimeout(() => process.exit(1), 5000).unref();
});

// DANGEROUS: treats every uncaught exception as recoverable, no exceptions
process.on('uncaughtException', (err) => {
  console.error('Error occurred, but continuing:', err.message);
  // no process.exit() -- the process just keeps running
});

let cache = {};

function updateCache(key, value) {
  cache[key].push(value);   // BUG: assumes cache[key] already exists as an array
}

updateCache('scores', 10);   // throws TypeError: Cannot read properties of undefined
// With the handler above, this error is logged and the process keeps running --
// but `cache` may now be missing an update it silently dropped, and every future
// caller of updateCache has no idea whether this specific call succeeded or not

